WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.789
When a request exceeds a limit, the application should respond predictably.

2
00:00:04.789 --> 00:00:11.814
The response should communicate that the request was limited without exposing sensitive implementation detail.

3
00:00:11.814 --> 00:00:21.584
Clients should be able to distinguish a limit response from an ambiguous application failure, and operators should be able to see which policy triggered.

4
00:00:21.584 --> 00:00:25.288
Retry guidance can be useful when it is clear and minimal.

5
00:00:25.288 --> 00:00:30.333
A client may need to know whether waiting before another request is reasonable.

6
00:00:30.333 --> 00:00:34.867
That guidance should be consistent with the policy and user experience.

7
00:00:34.867 --> 00:00:41.509
It should not reveal internal capacity details or create a noisy contract that the team cannot maintain.

8
00:00:41.509 --> 00:00:46.490
Queueing can smooth short bursts, but queued requests still consume resources.

9
00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:52.365
A queue may hold memory, threads, connection slots, request context, or downstream capacity.

10
00:00:52.365 --> 00:01:00.539
If a queue is unbounded or poorly configured, it can turn a short traffic spike into long latency and confusing client behavior.

11
00:01:00.539 --> 00:01:03.540
Queue limits should be bounded and intentional.

12
00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:13.502
Teams should decide whether queueing is appropriate for a given endpoint, how long users can reasonably wait, and what should happen when the queue is full.

13
00:01:13.502 --> 00:01:20.974
For some endpoints, quick rejection is more honest than making users wait for work that is unlikely to complete soon.

14
00:01:20.974 --> 00:01:26.721
Logging should capture limit events without exposing secrets or sensitive request content.

15
00:01:26.721 --> 00:01:35.342
Useful fields can include policy name, endpoint, route group, safe partition category, status, queue state, correlation ID, and timing.

16
00:01:35.342 --> 00:01:43.835
Avoid logging tokens, credentials, request bodies, uploaded file contents, or private identifiers that are not needed for operations.

17
00:01:43.835 --> 00:01:48.050
Good rejection behavior is part of user experience and operations.

18
00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:55.777
It protects the system, tells clients what happened, and gives operators enough signal to tune policies without guessing.
